/* Shared rules for the eight new-character-creator tabs (Tier 4 #4). Linked by
   every tab document AFTER theme.rcss so these win on equal specificity.

   ── CURSOR ROW LEGIBILITY — the reason this file exists ────────────────────────

   theme.rcss pairs `.item.selected` with an OPAQUE accent fill plus
   `color: {{bg-hard}}`, which assumes the row's text colour is the theme's to set.
   In this creator it is not: every row arrives through `data-rml`, so
   `cata_text_to_rml` has already baked the colour into an inline
   `<span style="color:…">` — and an inline colour beats a class rule.

   Measured on the POINTS tab: an RCSS `color` on the cursor row changed ZERO
   pixels, with and without `!important`, while an RCSS `background-color` applied
   immediately. So the intended dark-on-gold never happens — the semantic colour
   stays and sits on the gold fill at 1.65:1, well under the 4.5:1 legibility
   floor. The cursor row rendered as a blank gold bar on POINTS, SCENARIO and
   TRAITS. (STATS escaped only because its active colour happens to be dark.)

   Dropping the colour in the producer is NOT the fix: on TRAITS it is the only
   channel encoding taken / forbidden / available, and on PROFESSION and SCENARIO
   it encodes affordability. Stripping it would blind the user on the one row they
   are about to press Enter on.

   So mark the cursor with geometry and a DARK fill instead. Every other row
   already sits on a dark ground, so each semantic colour keeps exactly the
   contrast it was designed for — and the cursor row ends up the most readable row
   on screen rather than the least. */

.nc-opt, .nc-stat, .nc-skill-row, .nc-scen-row, .nc-prof-row, .nc-trait-row {
    /* Reserved on EVERY row, not just .selected, so the cursor's accent bar does
       not shift row text sideways as the cursor moves. Longhand, not the
       `border-left` shorthand: the shorthand form did not paint here. */
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: transparent;
}

.nc-opt.selected, .nc-stat.selected, .nc-skill-row.selected,
.nc-scen-row.selected, .nc-prof-row.selected, .nc-trait-row.selected {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-left-width: 3dp;
    border-left-color: {{yellow}};
    /* Bold as a second, colour-independent cursor cue — the fill step alone is
       easy to miss in the dense three-column TRAITS/BIONICS lists. */
    font-weight: bold;
    /* Overrides theme `.item.selected`'s on-accent (near-black), which would be
       invisible on this dark fill for any row that has NO baked colour. */
    color: {{fg0}};
}

/* ── GROUPING GEOMETRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

   The measured complaint is scannability, not spacing: the creator carries 2.5-4x
   the glyph-edge density of the UI_designs references (TRAITS 8.72% vs 1.36-2.55%)
   while its line leading is already comparable (1.41x vs 1.44-1.52x). Adding
   leading would not help; what is missing is anything telling the eye where one
   group ends.

   Only reaches what has an element boundary. Each OVERVIEW pane is a SINGLE div
   fed by one data-rml string with its header baked into the text ("Stats:\n
   Strength: 8\n..."), so a header cannot be given size or weight from here — that
   needs the producer to emit the header as its own element. See
   plans/charcreation-visual-overhaul.md workstream 4. */

/* Category headers in the skills list DO have an element boundary
   (data-class-header), so they can carry real hierarchy. */
.nc-skill-row.header {
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    margin-top: 10dp;
    margin-bottom: 2dp;
}

/* ── TAB STRIP GLYPHS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The theme's .tab is a plain text item; it now carries an icon + label, so it
   becomes a flex row. Icons are ?proc:runic-icon placeholders (see
   plans/charcreation-visual-overhaul.md for the art hand-off table). */
.nc-tabs .tab {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.nc-tab-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 16dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-tab-label {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* ── COLUMN HIERARCHY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   This is the hierarchy the screen was missing: previously every line in a column
   was the same 18dp weight, so a column title competed with its own data and the
   eye had nowhere to land. The title and group headings now have their own
   elements (see nc_desc_pane/nc_desc_row) and can carry weight. */
.nc-pane-head {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 6dp;
    padding-bottom: 3dp;
    border-bottom: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

.nc-pane-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 18dp;
    height: 18dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-pane-title {
    flex: 1;
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 2dp;
    color: {{fg0}};
}

.nc-pane-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: block;
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* Bottom gap so an overflowing pane's last row is not sliced flush against the
       pane edge. On a 1366x768-class terminal (170x48 cells) the panes are short
       enough to overflow, and without this the cut lands mid-glyph and reads as a
       rendering fault rather than as "scroll for more". */
    padding-bottom: 6dp;
}

.nc-pane-row {
    display: block;
}

/* Group headings inside a column (Spells:, Wielded:, a skill category). Spaced
   away from the rows above so the grouping is visible without a rule per group. */
.nc-pane-row.heading {
    font-weight: bold;
    margin-top: 8dp;
}

.nc-pane-row.heading:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
}

/* Values sit one luminance step above their labels, so a scan reads the DATA and
   the labels recede. Both are overridden per-row by the producer's baked colour
   where that colour is semantic (trait display colours, red None!). */
.nc-pane-label {
    color: {{fg4}};
}

.nc-pane-value {
    color: {{fg0}};
}

/* ── TRAIT / BIONIC BALANCE SCALE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   Replaces the "7/12 0/-12" formula, which stated the numbers without conveying
   whether you are leaning on advantages or disadvantages.

   NO ABSOLUTE POSITIONING. The first attempt floated the beam with
   `position: absolute` inside a `position: relative` .nc-scale; RmlUi resolved it
   against .nc-panel instead, putting the beam ~39dp above the pans and straight
   through the top bar. Everything here is flow layout: an arm row
   (pan | beam | pan) with the fulcrum centred beneath it.

   The BEAM rotates (transform from C++); the PANS do not ride it — they are
   siblings nudged by their own `top`, so the numbers stay upright instead of
   inheriting the rotation. `position: relative` on a pan makes that `top` a NUDGE
   from its flex position, so C++ never needs to know the layout's base offsets.
   Beam half-length 140dp and 8deg give an end rise of 140*sin(8) = 19.5dp, which is
   why nc_make_balance() nudges the pans by 20dp — the ends and the pans move
   together instead of visibly disagreeing.

   It sits in its own centred row: wedged into .nc-trait-top between the points and
   cost lines it read as clutter rather than as an indicator. */
.nc-balance-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 2dp 0 8dp 0;
}

.nc-scale {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    width: 340dp;
}

.nc-scale-arm {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    width: 100%;
    /* Tall enough to CONTAIN a pan at full tilt instead of relying on never reaching
       one. nc_scale::max_pan_dp() is 140*sin(8deg) = 19.5dp of travel each way, so a
       ~18dp text row needs 18 + 2*19.5 = 57dp. At 58dp with centred items a pan
       cannot escape into the neighbouring row at any balance value — the full-tilt
       state (one side of the budget spent, the other empty) is hard to reach by hand
       and so is a poor thing to leave to testing. */
    min-height: 58dp;
}

.nc-scale-beam {
    flex: 1;
    height: 2dp;
    margin: 0 6dp;
    background-color: {{yellow}};
    transition: transform 0.25s quadratic-out;
}

.nc-scale-pan {
    position: relative;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    transition: top 0.25s quadratic-out;
}

.nc-scale-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 16dp;
}

.nc-scale-num {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin: 0 4dp;
}

.nc-scale-fulcrum {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 14dp;
    height: 14dp;
}

/* ── WIZARD SHELL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Creation fills the screen. The panel is 98% with a 1% inset (set per-tab, since
   those sheets are linked after this one and win) so the panel's own border reads
   as a deliberate frame instead of running off the display edge.

   Inside it: a step rail across the top, then a row of [prev arrow | stage | next
   arrow], then the exit line. The arrows sit at the far left/right of the PANEL,
   which now IS the screen — the reference art has no visible panel edge, so the
   frame plus the inset is what keeps the composition from floating. */

/* Step rail. Reads as ordered progress rather than as tabs: a continuous hairline
   track with the current step underlined on it, and three brightness tiers for
   done / current / upcoming. */
.nc-steps {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    border-bottom: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

.nc-step {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 4dp 12dp;
    /* Reserved on every step so the current step's underline does not shift the
       rail's height as you advance. Longhand — the shorthand does not paint. */
    border-bottom-width: 2dp;
    border-bottom-color: transparent;
}

/* Upcoming: present but clearly not yet reached. */
.nc-step {
    color: {{fg4}};
}

/* Completed: brighter than upcoming, dimmer than current, so the rail shows how far
   along you are without competing with where you are. plain fg, not the dimmer fg2 — only fg,
   fg0 and fg4 exist in theme.json, and ui_theme::substitute_tokens turns an unknown
   token into #ff00ffff, i.e. magenta. */
.nc-step.done {
    color: {{fg}};
}

.nc-step.selected {
    color: {{yellow}};
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
    border-bottom-width: 2dp;
    border-bottom-color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-step-icon {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 16dp;
    height: 16dp;
    margin-right: 6dp;
}

.nc-step-label {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* Arrow row: the two navigators flank the working area. */
.nc-shell {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.nc-arrow {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 168dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 8dp;
    color: {{fg4}};
}

/* Hover is the click affordance: these are the only mouse targets in the shell. */
.nc-arrow:hover {
    color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-arrow-glyph {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 44dp;
}

.nc-arrow-key {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-top: 2dp;
}

.nc-arrow-dest {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    text-align: center;
    white-space: pre-wrap;
}

/* Keeps the stage centred when only one arrow is present (first and last step). */
.nc-arrow-spacer {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 168dp;
}

.nc-stage {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.nc-exit {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: block;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 6dp;
    color: {{fg4}};
}
